cartridge "METEOR STRIKE/COMPUTE! GAZETTE/1986"

' METEOR STRIKE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Meteor Strike", COMPUTE!'s Gazette July
' 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS2061; the author is not named on
' the disk). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026,
' in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/mstrike-code.dis, and timed in jsc64), the game is
' built for the ColecoVision: a starfield beside a grey panel with black
' label boxes as on the C64, a rocket in eight headings with a flame while
' it moves, two kinds of tumbling meteor, bursts, sound effects, a tune.
'
' The game: your rocket is caught in a meteor shower. Steer with the stick
' and shoot in the direction you last moved. A big meteor is worth 100, a
' small one 200. A meteor that touches the rocket costs one of five ships.
' Survive each level until LEVEL COMPLETE: every meteor left bursts (and
' scores) and the next level's meteors fly faster. Level 9 is the last and
' lasts until the ships run out; finishing level 8 gives a ship.
'
' From the original: six meteors, each with a speed of -3..+3 pixels a step
' on each axis (C64: -3..+4), wrapping round a 256 x 256 space of which
' the field is the visible part, so a meteor leaving one side comes back
' on the other after a while; the meteors' step clock speeds up every
' level (C64: a delay of 12 - level); a hit meteor bursts for 5 of its
' steps, then a new one (big or small at random) comes in from an edge;
' one shot at a time, flying in the rocket's last heading until it leaves
' the field (holding the button fires again at once); a hit rocket bursts,
' everything stops a moment, then the meteors start again from the edges
' and the rocket from the middle; 5 ships, a ship for finishing level 8,
' level 9 the last; the level-end burst scores every meteor in flight.
'
' Liberties: the field is 168 x 176 pixels (C64 about 208 x 180) and the
' speeds are scaled to it and slowed for human players (meteors from 0.20
' steps a frame at level 1 to 0.61 at level 9; the C64 went from about
' 0.3 to 1 step of up to 4 pixels); the rocket moves 1.5 pixels a frame
' (C64 about 2) and stays inside the field (on the C64 it wrapped through
' the invisible border like the meteors); a new meteor always flies into
' the field from the edge it starts at and never stands still (the C64
' picked any speed, including none); a meteor is replaced half way round
' the unseen part of the space (the C64 did it where X or Y passed 0);
' every other new meteor (after a burst or such a replacement) is aimed
' at where the rocket is as it comes in, so that standing still or
' circling does not pay (the C64's meteors flew at random); hits are box
' tests on the drawn shapes (the C64 used sprite collisions); a level
' lasts 25 seconds (about 24 in jsc64); the shot flies 4 pixels a frame;
' the score stops at 999900 (the C64 rolled over); no pause key (the C64
' paused with SHIFT); GAME OVER then the title (the C64 waited for the
' button and restarted).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the rocket, either side
' button fires. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

